WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.478
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:15.449
Unit twenty-eight: Children's Literature. Today you will describe past habits with 'used to' and 'would'.

4
00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.273
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.501
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:24.757
Children's literature.

7
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.911
Children's literature means books written for young readers.

8
00:00:29.517 --> 00:00:32.399
Children's literature can deal with serious themes.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:35.926
Your turn. Say: Children's literature.

10
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.290
Children's literature.

11
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.132
Picture book.

12
00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:47.160
Picture book means a book in which illustrations are as important as the words.

13
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.075
Picture books build early literacy.

14
00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.127
Your turn. Say: Picture book.

15
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:57.037
Picture book.

16
00:00:57.783 --> 00:00:58.564
Moral.

17
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.072
Moral means the lesson that a story teaches.

18
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:05.937
The moral of the fable is 'slow and steady wins the race'.

19
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.819
Your turn. Say: Moral.

20
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:12.459
Moral.

21
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:14.357
Imagination.

22
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.196
Imagination means the ability to form new images and ideas.

23
00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:22.630
Fantasy stories stretch a child's imagination.

24
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:26.264
Your turn. Say: Imagination.

25
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.275
Imagination.

26
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:32.192
Illustration.

27
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:35.787
Illustration means a picture in a book.

28
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:38.638
The illustrations tell a second story.

29
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.856
Your turn. Say: Illustration.

30
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.933
Illustration.

31
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:47.940
Fairy tale.

32
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.540
Fairy tale means a traditional story with magic and fantasy.

33
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.765
Every culture has its own fairy tales.

34
00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:59.339
Your turn. Say: Fairy tale.

35
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.375
Fairy tale.

36
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:05.129
Young reader.

37
00:02:06.117 --> 00:02:08.882
Young reader means a child who reads or is read to.

38
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:11.460
Young readers love repetition.

39
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.551
Your turn. Say: Young reader.

40
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.517
Young reader.

41
00:02:19.283 --> 00:02:20.319
Values.

42
00:02:21.317 --> 00:02:24.199
Values means principles about what is important in life.

43
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:27.224
Stories pass on a community's values.

44
00:02:27.817 --> 00:02:30.759
Your turn. Say: Values.

45
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.740
Values.

46
00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:39.196
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.152
Visal, what kinds of stories did you hear as a child?

48
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:52.231
My grandmother would tell me Khmer folk tales every night. She used to act out the voices of the rabbit and the crocodile.

49
00:02:53.317 --> 00:02:55.432
Did those stories have clear morals?

50
00:02:56.517 --> 00:03:06.425
Very clear. The clever rabbit would always outwit the stronger animals, which taught us that intelligence matters more than strength.

51
00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:10.399
Do you think those values still matter for children today?

52
00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:22.843
Absolutely. Children used to learn them by listening; now they often learn them from picture books and cartoons, but the lessons haven't changed.

53
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.465
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:30.209
Grammar focus: Used to and would for past habits.

55
00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:31.707
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:34.973
Parents would read fairy tales at bedtime.

57
00:03:35.883 --> 00:03:38.603
My grandmother used to act out the animal voices.

58
00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:42.619
The clever rabbit would always outwit the stronger animals.

59
00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:54.151
Both 'used to' and 'would' describe repeated past actions. Only 'used to' can describe past STATES: 'I used to love fairy tales' (not 'I would love'). 'Would' is common in nostalgic storytelling.

60
00:03:54.917 --> 00:03:59.160
Be careful! Don't say: Parents would to read fairy tales at night.

61
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.773
Say: Parents would read fairy tales at night.

62
00:04:04.073 --> 00:04:06.131
After 'would', use the base verb.

63
00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:10.062
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.867 --> 00:04:12.843
More Than Bedtime Stories.

65
00:04:13.143 --> 00:04:18.660
Children's literature is often dismissed as simple, yet it plays a powerful role in shaping values.

66
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:25.021
In many cultures, elders would pass on moral lessons through animal fables and fairy tales.

67
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:31.237
These stories used to be transmitted orally, adapting slightly with each new teller and audience.

68
00:04:32.233 --> 00:04:37.611
Today, picture books combine words and illustrations to develop both literacy and imagination.

69
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:44.930
Critics also examine what such books teach implicitly, from gender roles to attitudes toward other cultures.

70
00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:49.919
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.783 --> 00:04:53.456
I blank love scary stories when I was small.

72
00:04:53.756 --> 00:04:57.236
A, would. B, used to. C, was used to.

73
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.776
The answer is B: used to.

74
00:05:03.917 --> 00:05:06.543
Every evening, my father blank read to us.

75
00:05:06.843 --> 00:05:09.963
A, would. B, was. C, uses to.

76
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.896
The answer is A: would.

77
00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:18.479
The lesson a story teaches is its blank.

78
00:05:18.779 --> 00:05:23.245
A, moral. B, illustration. C, fairy tale.

79
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.474
The answer is A: moral.

80
00:05:29.617 --> 00:05:31.314
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:31.614 --> 00:05:37.578
A, She would to tell stories.. B, She used tell stories.. C, She used to tell stories..

82
00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:43.597
The answer is C: She used to tell stories..

83
00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.468
According to the reading, how were these stories once transmitted?

84
00:05:48.768 --> 00:05:53.025
A, orally. B, online. C, through films.

85
00:05:56.567 --> 00:05:58.346
The answer is A: orally.

86
00:05:59.533 --> 00:06:02.580
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:03.417 --> 00:06:06.043
How did the stories you read as a child shape you?

88
00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:24.408
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:55.316
The stories I heard as a child shaped my values more than I realised at the time. My mother would read me Khmer fables in which the small, clever rabbit always defeated stronger animals. These stories taught me that thinking carefully can be more powerful than physical strength. I also used to love stories about loyal friends, which made me value friendship. Some people believe children's stories are too simple to matter. I disagree, because the earliest lessons are often the deepest.

90
00:06:55.616 --> 00:06:57.244
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:02.927
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and used to and would for past habits.

92
00:07:03.227 --> 00:07:06.971
Today's value is love of reading. Read a book every day.

93
00:07:07.271 --> 00:07:10.144
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:10.444 --> 00:07:12.840
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

